b/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078568 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1045 "Approximating Functions Using Local Linearity and Linearization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078647 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "580243c0-444d-51d9-820f-ab39d2e434fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079512 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079512 #edb/ref 1061 4825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079512 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80d363a5-ade6-554f-b554-78362a2a4a87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079512 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079513 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079513 #edb/ref 1061 4826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079513 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29daedd8-9104-56cf-8960-bc6517ab9536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079513 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079514 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079514 #edb/ref 1061 4829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079514 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f65d689-6eed-51f9-99e7-de9d4bff5796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079514 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079515 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079515 #edb/ref 1061 4832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079515 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "539a8b72-5dd7-5bd3-b6ff-ecd4fb4535ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079515 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079516 #edb/ref 1061 4833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079516 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a4e1771-2f14-58d7-9a7d-62eb186154dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079516 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ below. Suppose we know that $f(2)=5$ and $f'(2)=1.$ Based on this information, how can we approximate the value of $f(2.75)?$\n\n![](images/25/25baabc5d4193a480d09370faa104d34edc14b2aa09759f5976d592a387fb398.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\ny-f(a) = f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the above, we get \n\n$$\ny= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $x$ is close to $a,$ we can assume that $y\\approx f(x) ,$ which gives us the approximation\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nWe often represent the tangent line approximation using the notation $L(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we want the tangent line approximation at $a=2.$ Substituting this value along with the given information, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =(5)+(1)(x-2) \\\\  & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the graphs. Notice that in the neighborhood of $x=2$, the function and its tangent line approximation are very close. The line $L(x)$ is a good approximation of the function for points close to $x=2.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1de31c316c019e8faf466f64a0e6ff24a29935a7ec354c2acdf18d3069b8d47.png)\n\nAs we can see, ${\\color{blue}L(2.75)}$ and ${\\color{red}f(2.75)}$ are quite close. So, we can approximate $f(2.75)$ using $L(2.75)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.75) & ≈L(2.75) \\\\  & =(2.75)+3 \\\\  & =5.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line approximation\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nis sometimes also called the **linear approximation** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a.$ The process of approximating the function by its tangent is called **linearization.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1065 579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fd16cbf-e232-56c9-84ba-d6b6ff2d147a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $L(x)$ be the tangent line approximation of $y = f(x)$ at $x=a,$ as shown in the pictures below. \n\n| ![](images/ad/ad7375c544eed881d96d07b449d9003c7df813bdddcdaf29e82bfda6bd4cb283.png) | ![](images/b5/b5b3cd71da3243a5848021831403adde07f1487ff1077f3359d2564ba0f4b686.png) |\n| --- | --- |\n\nWe can make the following conclusions:\n\n- When the function is *concave up*, the tangent line is *below* the graph (see the picture on the left). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\lt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *underestimate* of the function.\n\n- When the function is *concave down*, the tangent line is *above* the graph (see the picture on the right). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\gt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *overestimate* of the function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1065 582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "717ddca8-0e12-5d53-9384-635de0a676ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1067 "Underestimate and Overestimate Approximations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083240 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1